(Before your agent can use event triggers in production, you need to understand) Tj
T*
(authentication and security implications.) Tj
T*
T*
(Maker authentication) Tj
T*
T*
(Event triggers use the agent creator's credentials for all authentication:) Tj
T*
T*
(- Your agent accesses systems using your permissions) Tj
T*
(- Users can potentially access data through your credentials) Tj
T*
(- All actions are performed "as you" even when users interact with the agent) Tj
T*
T*
(Data protection best practices) Tj
T*
T*
(To maintain security when publishing agents with event triggers:) Tj
T*
T*
(1. Evaluate data access - Review what systems and data your triggers can access) Tj
T*
(1. Test thoroughly - Understand what information triggers include in payloads) Tj
T*
(1. Narrow trigger scope - Use specific parameters to limit what events activate triggers) Tj
T*
(1. Review payload data - Ensure triggers don't expose sensitive information) Tj
T*
(1. Monitor usage - Track trigger activity and resource consumption) Tj
T*
T*
(   Troubleshooting and limitations) Tj
T*
T*
(Keep these important considerations in mind when working with event triggers:) Tj
T*
T*
(Quota and billing impacts) Tj
T*
T*
(- Each trigger activation counts toward your message consumption) Tj
T*
(- Frequent triggers \(like every-minute recurrence\) can quickly consume quota) Tj
T*
(- Monitor usage to avoid throttling) Tj
T*
T*
(Technical requirements) Tj
T*
T*
(- Only available for agents with generative orchestration enabled) Tj
T*
(- Requires solution-aware cloud flow sharing to be enabled in your environment) Tj
T*
T*
(Data Loss Prevention \(DLP\)) Tj
T*
T*
(- Your organization's DLP policies determine which triggers are available) Tj
T*
(- Administrators can block event triggers entirely) Tj
T*
(- Contact your admin if expected triggers aren't available) Tj
T*
T*
(  Lab 10 - Add Event Triggers for autonomous agent behavior) Tj
T*
T*
(  Use case) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
